      "shift": "24 Jun 2022, Shift 1",
      "status": "draft",
      "stem": "One mole of a monoatomic gas is mixed with three moles of a diatomic gas. What is the molecular specific heat of the mixture at constant volume ($C_v$)? (Take $R$ as the universal gas constant)",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "content": "$\\frac{9}{4} R$",
          "is_correct": true
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "content": "$\\frac{3}{2} R$",
          "is_correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "content": "$\\frac{5}{2} R$",
          "is_correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "content": "$\\frac{13}{4} R$",
          "is_correct": false
        }
      ],
      "solution": "For monoatomic gas: $n_1 = 1$, $C_{v1} = \\frac{3}{2}R$.\nFor diatomic gas: $n_2 = 3$, $C_{v2} = \\frac{5}{2}R$.\nFor the mixture:\n$C_{v\\text{, mix}} = \\frac{n_1 C_{v1} + n_2 C_{v2}}{n_1 + n_2} = \\frac{1 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}R + 3 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2}R}{1 + 3} = \\frac{\\frac{3}{2}R + \\frac{15}{2}R}{4} = \\frac{9R}{4}$."
    },
    {
      "ref": "PHY-KTG-002",
      "subtopic": "degrees-of-freedom-and-law-of-equipartition-of-energy",
